window.AVIATION_Q26_100=[["What is ticket revalidation?","Ticket revalidation is the process of updating eligible flight information on an existing electronic ticket **without issuing a completely new ticket**, when permitted.","Revalidation may be possible when certain flight details change but the fare and ticket do not require a full exchange. Whether revalidation is allowed depends on the airline, ticket, fare conditions, and type of change. It should not be treated as an alternative to reissue in every situation. A professional agent first checks the ticket status, fare rules, new itinerary, and carrier policy. In an interview, say: **“Revalidation updates eligible ticket coupon information without a full reissue, but I would first verify that the airline and fare conditions permit it.”**"],
["What is the difference between reissue and revalidation?","A **reissue creates a replacement ticket through an exchange**, whereas **revalidation updates eligible information on the existing ticket without a full reissue**.","Reissue is generally required when a change affects ticketed information in a way that requires recalculation or exchange, such as a fare difference or certain itinerary changes. Revalidation is more limited and is only available when the airline and ticket conditions permit it. An agent should never decide between the two simply from memory. The correct method depends on the carrier's procedures and the ticket. In an interview, emphasize that you would check the **fare conditions, ticket status, airline policy, and nature of the change** before deciding whether reissue or revalidation applies."],
["What is a refund?","A refund is the return of the **eligible unused value of a ticket or related document** according to the applicable fare rules and airline policy.","When a passenger cancels travel, the agent must determine whether the ticket is refundable and what deductions apply. A refund can depend on the fare conditions, ticket status, used or unused coupons, cancellation penalty, no-show status, taxes, and airline policy. A non-refundable fare does not necessarily mean every component of the ticket is always non-refundable; applicable taxes and regulatory requirements can vary. Therefore, an agent should never promise an amount before checking the ticket. A strong interview response is: **“I would verify eligibility and calculate the applicable refund before confirming the amount to the passenger.”**"],
["What is a refundable ticket?","A refundable ticket is a ticket whose fare conditions permit an eligible refund, usually subject to the applicable rules and possible deductions.","“Refundable” does not always mean that the passenger automatically receives the entire amount paid. Depending on the fare, there can still be cancellation charges, no-show penalties, used portions, or other adjustments. The agent should review the fare conditions and ticket status before quoting the passenger. Refundability can also differ between fare components in a complex itinerary. During an interview, avoid saying, **“Refundable means a full refund.”** Instead say: **“A refundable fare allows an eligible refund according to its conditions, and I would calculate any applicable deductions before confirming the final amount.”**"],
["What is a non-refundable ticket?","A non-refundable ticket is a ticket whose fare conditions generally restrict the refund of the applicable fare amount after cancellation.","A non-refundable ticket does not mean that the agent should immediately tell the passenger, “You will receive nothing.” Depending on the ticket, jurisdiction, taxes, airline policy, or disruption circumstances, some amounts may still require separate assessment. There may also be options such as changes or credits depending on the fare and airline. Therefore, the correct approach is to check the actual fare rules and ticket status. In an interview, say: **“I would explain the restriction clearly, check whether any eligible taxes or other value can be returned, and offer permitted alternatives where available.”**"],
["What is a voluntary change?","A voluntary change is a change requested by the passenger for personal reasons rather than because of an airline disruption.","For example, a passenger may decide to travel one day later because their meeting has changed. This is a voluntary change. The agent should check the original fare conditions, new flight availability, fare difference, change penalty, ticket validity, and any other applicable restrictions. Voluntary changes usually follow the ticket's normal fare rules unless an authorized exception exists. In an interview, explain that the agent should not simply change the flight. The complete process is **verify the ticket → check rules → find availability → calculate charges → obtain customer agreement → complete the authorized servicing transaction**."],
["What is an involuntary change?","An involuntary change occurs when travel is affected by an airline or operational disruption rather than by the passenger's voluntary decision.","Examples may include flight cancellation, certain schedule changes, or disrupted connections. In these situations, normal voluntary change penalties may not apply in the same way because the airline can issue specific reaccommodation or waiver instructions. The exact solution depends on the carrier's policy and circumstances. An agent should check the disruption details and authorized policy before rebooking, reissuing, or refunding. In an interview, say: **“For an involuntary change, I would review the disruption and current airline waiver or reaccommodation policy before offering the passenger eligible alternatives.”**"],
["What is additional collection?","Additional collection is an extra amount that the passenger may need to pay during a ticket exchange or reissue.","When a passenger changes travel, the new applicable fare can be higher than the value available from the original ticket. There may also be an applicable change penalty, tax difference, or other authorized amount. The resulting amount due is collected before completing the ticketing transaction. Agents should never quote an additional collection without completing the required calculation. In an interview, explain that **additional collection is not automatically the same as a change fee**. It can reflect the overall amount due after recalculating the new itinerary according to the fare rules and ticket exchange procedure."],
["What is a no-show?","A no-show generally occurs when a passenger fails to present for the booked flight as required and does not cancel or change the reservation within the applicable deadline.","The consequences of a no-show depend on the airline and fare conditions. A no-show may result in additional penalties, loss of certain ticket value, or cancellation of subsequent segments, depending on the carrier's rules. Therefore, it is incorrect to say that every no-show ticket automatically becomes void. If a customer contacts support after missing a flight, the agent should check the reservation, ticket status, remaining itinerary, and no-show conditions before offering a solution. This is an important technical and customer-service question in airline interviews."],
["What is a gate no-show?","A gate no-show generally refers to a passenger who does not board the flight by the required boarding or gate deadline despite being expected for the flight.","This differs operationally from a passenger who never presents for check-in. For example, a passenger may complete check-in but fail to arrive at the boarding gate before it closes. Operational procedures may then be required, including baggage handling where applicable. From a customer-service perspective, the agent should never promise that the passenger can simply board another flight free of charge. The ticket status, no-show conditions, remaining itinerary, and airline policy must first be reviewed."],
["What is a split PNR?","A split PNR is created when one or more passengers are separated from a multi-passenger reservation into another reservation record.","Suppose a husband and wife are booked together, but the husband becomes ill and needs to change or cancel his travel while the wife continues as planned. The passengers may need to be separated so that one passenger's itinerary can be serviced independently. Splitting the reservation does **not itself refund or cancel the ticket**. After the split, the relevant passenger's reservation and ticket must be handled according to the applicable rules. This distinction is important because an interviewer may intentionally ask whether splitting automatically cancels the passenger's journey."],
["Why would you split a PNR?","A PNR may be split when passengers booked together need to have their reservations serviced differently.","For example, four family members may be travelling together, but one person needs to change the departure date while the other three continue on the original flight. Keeping everyone in the same record can make individual servicing difficult or inappropriate. By separating the relevant passenger, the agent can work on that person's itinerary without unnecessarily changing the others. However, splitting should be performed carefully because linked services, seats, special requests, ticket information, and other booking elements may require review. After the split, both resulting records should be checked for accuracy and completeness."],
["Does splitting a PNR automatically cancel the ticket?","**No.** Splitting a PNR separates passengers into different reservation records; it does not automatically cancel or refund their tickets.","This is an important distinction. The reservation and the electronic ticket are related but separate records. After splitting passengers, an agent may still need to cancel segments, rebook travel, reissue a ticket, or process an eligible refund according to the passenger's request and fare conditions. Therefore, saying “split PNR means ticket cancellation” would be incorrect. A strong interview response is: **“I would first split the relevant passenger where appropriate, verify both PNRs, and then service the separated passenger's ticket according to the applicable fare rules and airline policy.”**"],
["What is SSR?","SSR stands for **Special Service Request**.","An SSR is structured information used to request or communicate a particular passenger-related service that requires airline processing. Common examples can include wheelchair assistance, certain special meals, infant information, and other services depending on the carrier. An SSR should not be treated as a guarantee simply because it has been requested; its status and airline confirmation may need to be checked. The exact SSR codes and acceptance conditions can vary by service and airline. In an interview, explain that SSR is used when the passenger requires a **specific service or handling that needs to be communicated through the reservation system**."],
["What is OSI?","OSI stands for **Other Service Information**.","OSI is used to communicate relevant information to an airline that does not function in the same way as a structured special service request. It can provide information that the carrier should be aware of without necessarily requesting a specific service requiring confirmation. The exact use depends on the airline and reservation environment. The key interview distinction is: **SSR requests or communicates a structured service requirement, whereas OSI primarily communicates relevant information.** Agents should use the correct element because an incorrectly entered service request can lead to the passenger not receiving the required assistance."],
["What is the difference between SSR and OSI?","SSR is generally used for a **specific service request requiring airline processing**, while OSI provides **other relevant information to the airline**.","For example, a wheelchair request would normally be communicated through an appropriate SSR because the airline needs to arrange assistance. An OSI, on the other hand, may communicate relevant information without functioning as a service request. The distinction matters because SSRs can have status codes indicating whether the service has been confirmed or otherwise processed. In an interview, avoid saying simply, “SSR needs action and OSI does not.” The more precise explanation is that they have **different structured purposes within airline reservation messaging and servicing workflows**."],
["What is WCHR?","WCHR is a wheelchair-assistance category generally used for a passenger who needs assistance for distance but can manage stairs and reach the cabin seat.","Wheelchair SSR categories communicate different levels of passenger mobility assistance. WCHR generally represents the least intensive of the common WCHR/WCHS/WCHC categories. However, agents should not select a code based only on assumptions about the passenger. They should ask appropriate mobility questions according to the airline's procedure so that the correct assistance can be arranged. This is important for safety, dignity, and operational preparation. In an interview, demonstrate that wheelchair codes are not merely memorized abbreviations; they communicate the **level of assistance the passenger requires**."],
["What is WCHS?","WCHS generally indicates a passenger who cannot manage stairs but can make their way to or from the cabin seat once appropriately assisted.","This category requires more assistance than WCHR because stairs are a limitation for the passenger. The airport and airline may therefore need to arrange appropriate boarding or disembarkation assistance. An agent should ask the passenger suitable mobility questions and record the correct service according to airline procedures. Never make assumptions based on age, appearance, or medical condition. The objective is to identify the passenger's actual mobility needs. During an interview, clearly differentiate WCHS from WCHR: **WCHR can generally manage stairs, whereas WCHS generally cannot manage stairs.**"],
["What is WCHC?","WCHC generally indicates a passenger who requires more complete wheelchair assistance, including assistance to or from the aircraft seat.","Among the commonly discussed wheelchair categories, WCHC generally represents a passenger with a higher level of mobility assistance requirement. The exact operational handling is governed by the airline and airport procedures. The agent should obtain appropriate information respectfully and avoid asking unnecessary medical questions outside the approved process. Correct SSR selection is essential because airport teams need accurate information to prepare the required assistance. For interview purposes, remember: **WCHR – distance assistance; WCHS – cannot manage stairs; WCHC – requires assistance to/from the cabin seat**, subject to carrier procedures."],
["What is VGML?","VGML is a commonly used special-meal code for a **vegetarian vegan meal**.","Special meal codes allow airlines to identify eligible passenger meal requirements in advance. A VGML generally excludes meat, fish, dairy, and eggs, although exact catering specifications follow the airline's meal standards. Agents should never guarantee a special meal simply because a request was entered. Availability, advance-request deadlines, route, cabin, and airline confirmation can affect whether it is provided. When a passenger requests a special meal, the agent should select the correct service code, check whether the airline offers it, and confirm the status according to procedure."],
["What is INFT?","INFT is an airline reservation code associated with an **infant passenger**.","Infant bookings require special handling because airlines have specific rules concerning age, accompanying adults, documentation, fares, seating, and safety. An infant is not simply added as a normal adult passenger. The booking must correctly associate the infant with the accompanying passenger according to the carrier's procedure. International journeys may also require appropriate travel documents. If an interviewer asks about INFT, do not stop at the abbreviation. Explain that infant reservations require careful verification of the **date of birth, accompanying adult, applicable fare, documentation, and airline-specific conditions**."],
["What is a purged PNR?","A purged PNR is a reservation record that is no longer available as an active PNR after the applicable system retention period or lifecycle.","After travel has been completed and sufficient time has passed, a reservation may no longer remain accessible through the normal live-PNR retrieval process. Historical or archived information may require a different retrieval method, subject to system capability, airline policy, and data-retention rules. Therefore, “purged” does not necessarily mean that every trace of the reservation has permanently disappeared. In an interview, say: **“A purged PNR is no longer available through normal active retrieval, and historical information may require the appropriate archive or past-date retrieval process where available.”**"],
["What is PNR history?","PNR history is the record of changes and transactions that have occurred within a reservation.","PNR history can help an agent investigate what happened to a booking. Depending on the system and access, it may show information relating to segment changes, cancellations, contact updates, received-from elements, splits, and other transactions. This is especially useful when a passenger disputes a change or claims that a booking was modified without authorization. However, agents must interpret history correctly rather than jumping to conclusions from one entry. In an interview, explain that PNR history provides an **audit trail of reservation activity**, making it useful for investigation, quality control, and servicing disputes."],
["What does “Received From” mean in a PNR?","The Received From element records who requested or authorized the reservation transaction or change.","This element contributes to the audit trail of the PNR. When an agent modifies a reservation, it is important to record who provided the instruction according to the system and company procedure. For example, the request may come from the passenger or another authorized source. This becomes useful when reviewing PNR history later. The Received From element does not replace required identity verification or authorization procedures. In an interview, explain that it records the **source of the transaction instruction**, helping maintain accountability and traceability within the reservation record."],
["What is MCT?","MCT stands for **Minimum Connecting Time**.","MCT is the minimum published connection time required for a passenger to transfer between eligible flights in a particular airport and connection scenario. It can vary depending on whether the journey is domestic or international, the terminals involved, airlines, airports, and other factors. Agents should not simply estimate whether a connection “looks long enough.” The applicable connection requirement must be checked. If an itinerary does not satisfy the relevant MCT, it can create an invalid or impractical connection. In an interview, say: **“MCT helps determine whether the planned connection provides the minimum required transfer time.”**"],
["Why is MCT important?","MCT is important because it helps determine whether a passenger has the minimum required time to make a valid flight connection.","Connecting passengers may need to disembark, change terminals, complete security or immigration formalities, and reach the next boarding gate. The required time differs by airport and itinerary. A connection below the applicable MCT can create a high risk of misconnection and may not be valid for ticketing. Agents should therefore review MCT when creating or changing connecting itineraries. In an interview, explain that MCT is an operational minimum, not a guarantee that every passenger will comfortably make the connection under every real-world circumstance."],
["What is IROP?","IROP stands for **Irregular Operations**.","Irregular operations refer to disruptions that affect normal flight operations. Examples can include cancellations, significant delays, diversions, or missed connections caused by operational events. During IROPs, agents often need to assist affected passengers with alternative flights, rebooking, ticket servicing, or refunds according to the airline's disruption policy. Normal voluntary fare rules may be modified by specific waivers or reaccommodation instructions. In an interview, explain that during IROPs your priorities are to **understand the disruption, verify the passenger's itinerary, check the authorized policy, identify eligible alternatives, and communicate clearly and empathetically**."],
["How would you handle a passenger affected by IROPs?","I would first verify the booking and disruption, then check the airline's current reaccommodation or waiver policy before offering eligible alternatives.","I would listen to the passenger's requirements and check the complete itinerary, including onward connections. I would then search for permitted alternatives and explain them clearly. If the passenger accepts an option, I would follow the authorized rebooking and ticket-servicing procedure and verify the updated booking. If eligible refund options exist, I would explain those accurately as well. I would avoid making promises outside policy. I would also acknowledge the inconvenience because disrupted passengers can be stressed. This answer demonstrates **customer service, ticketing awareness, policy compliance, and problem-solving**."],
["What is a codeshare flight?","A codeshare flight is a flight operated by one airline but marketed or sold under the flight code of another participating airline.","For example, Airline A may operate the aircraft while Airline B sells seats on the same physical flight using its own flight number. This allows airlines to expand the destinations they can market without physically operating every flight themselves. For passengers, codeshares can affect check-in, seat selection, baggage information, terminal details, and servicing. Therefore, agents should identify both the marketing and operating carrier. In an interview, explain: **“The marketing carrier sells the flight under its code, while the operating carrier actually operates the aircraft.”**"],
["What is the difference between a marketing carrier and an operating carrier?","The **marketing carrier sells the flight under its airline code**, while the **operating carrier physically operates the flight**.","On a codeshare itinerary, the passenger may see one airline's flight number even though another airline operates the aircraft. This distinction is important because some services are controlled by the operating carrier. Check-in arrangements, baggage procedures, seat selection, onboard service, and operational information may depend on which airline operates the flight. Agents should clearly explain this to passengers when necessary. In an interview, provide a conceptual example: **“If QR markets a flight but another partner operates it, QR is the marketing carrier and the partner is the operating carrier.”**"],
["What is an interline agreement?","An interline agreement allows participating airlines to cooperate in specified areas of passenger travel, such as ticketing and baggage handling, subject to their agreement.","Interline arrangements can make journeys involving multiple airlines easier because participating carriers may accept each other's ticketing or baggage arrangements within agreed conditions. However, not every airline has an interline agreement with every other airline, and the exact scope can differ. Interline should also not be confused with codeshare. In a codeshare, multiple airline codes can be associated with the same operated flight. Interline more broadly concerns cooperation between carriers across an itinerary. In an interview, emphasize that the actual agreement and ticketing rules must always be verified."],
["What is the difference between codeshare and interline?","Codeshare involves airlines marketing the same operated flight under different flight numbers, while interline allows airlines to cooperate across passenger journeys under agreed arrangements.","With codeshare, one airline operates the flight while another can market that same flight under its own code. With interline, the passenger may travel on separate flights operated by different airlines, with cooperation in areas such as ticketing or baggage where permitted. The two concepts can overlap but are not identical. A good interview answer is: **“Codeshare is primarily about shared marketing of a flight; interline is about cooperation between airlines across an itinerary.”** Always verify the specific airline agreements before advising passengers."],
["What is a transit passenger?","A transit passenger is a passenger travelling through an intermediate airport on the way to a final destination.","The exact immigration and operational treatment of a transit passenger depends on the airport, country, itinerary, terminal, nationality, and whether the passenger must enter the country or remain airside. Therefore, an agent should never make broad visa promises based only on the word “transit.” For example, some passengers may require a transit visa while others may not, depending on their circumstances. In an interview, explain the general concept but add that **visa, immigration, baggage, and connection requirements must be checked against authoritative rules for the specific passenger and itinerary**."],
["What is a connecting flight?","A connecting flight is a subsequent flight used by a passenger to continue from an intermediate point to the final destination.","For example, a passenger travelling from Delhi to London via Doha takes DEL–DOH first and then DOH–LHR as the connecting flight. Connections require attention to MCT, terminals, baggage, ticketing arrangements, immigration requirements, and operational status. Agents should review the complete itinerary rather than treating each flight independently. If the first flight is delayed, the onward connection may be affected. In an interview, explain that a connecting itinerary contains **multiple flight segments that work together to transport the passenger from the origin to the final destination**."],
["What is a layover?","A layover is the period a passenger spends at an intermediate point between two flight segments.","For example, if a passenger arrives in Doha at 10:00 and departs for London at 13:00, there is a three-hour connection period in Doha. Whether a connection is described as a layover or stopover for fare purposes can depend on fare construction and applicable rules, so the terms should not always be treated as technically interchangeable. During the connection, passengers may need to follow transfer procedures, security requirements, or immigration rules depending on the itinerary. Agents should also ensure that the connection meets the applicable minimum connecting time."],
["What are IATA Traffic Conference Areas?","IATA tariff geography traditionally divides the world into **Traffic Conference Areas 1, 2, and 3**, commonly called TC1, TC2, and TC3.","These areas are used in international air-fare geography and related industry concepts. They should not be confused with continents because the boundaries are designed for aviation tariff purposes. TC1 broadly relates to the Americas/Western Hemisphere, TC2 includes Europe, Africa, and the Middle East, and TC3 includes major parts of Asia and the Pacific. Understanding these areas is useful in airline fare and geography interviews. Instead of memorizing only the numbers, learn which major regions belong to each traffic conference area and practise identifying the area for common countries."],
["What is TC1?","TC1 broadly covers the **Americas and associated areas in the Western Hemisphere** under IATA tariff geography.","North America, Central America, South America, and relevant associated areas fall within Traffic Conference Area 1 under IATA's tariff-geography framework. For interview preparation, remember major countries such as the United States, Canada, Brazil, Argentina, and Mexico as TC1 examples. Do not confuse TC1 with the geographical continent of North America alone. It is an aviation traffic-conference concept covering a much broader region. Interviewers may ask you to identify the TC area of a country, so practising representative countries from each area is more useful than memorizing a vague definition."],
["What is TC2?","TC2 broadly includes **Europe, Africa, and the Middle East**.","Examples include the United Kingdom, France, Germany, the United Arab Emirates, Qatar, Saudi Arabia, Egypt, Kenya, and South Africa. This classification is used in IATA tariff geography and should not be confused with political organizations such as the European Union or Schengen Area. For example, Qatar and the UAE are geographically in the Middle East and fall within TC2 for this framework. A simple memory rule is **TC2 = Europe + Africa + Middle East**. Interviewers often combine traffic-conference questions with routes, airport codes, and fare geography."],
["What is TC3?","TC3 broadly covers major areas of **Asia and the Pacific**, including the South Asian Subcontinent.","India belongs to TC3. Other examples include many countries and territories across East Asia, Southeast Asia, South Asia, Australia, New Zealand, and Pacific regions within the IATA tariff framework. For interview purposes, remember major destinations such as India, Japan, Singapore, Thailand, Malaysia, and Australia as TC3 examples. The precise IATA tariff definitions are more detailed than simply “Asia and Australia,” so for technical work you should consult current industry references. For interview preparation, the essential point is that **India is in TC3, not TC2**."],
["Which Traffic Conference Area does India belong to?","**India belongs to TC3**, specifically within the South Asian Subcontinent area of IATA tariff geography.","This is a common aviation geography interview question. Candidates sometimes incorrectly place India in TC2 because it lies west of much of East Asia. However, IATA tariff geography is not based purely on simple east-west geographic divisions. India is classified within TC3. A useful way to remember it is: **Europe/Middle East/Africa = TC2; India and much of Asia-Pacific = TC3**. If the interviewer asks a follow-up question about travel from India to Dubai, you can explain that the journey crosses from **TC3 to TC2**."],
["Which Traffic Conference Area does the UAE belong to?","The **United Arab Emirates belongs to TC2**, as it is part of the Middle East within IATA tariff geography.","Dubai and Abu Dhabi are major aviation hubs, so this is a useful classification to remember. A journey from India to the UAE therefore moves from TC3 to TC2. Do not confuse geographic proximity with traffic-conference classification. Although Dubai is relatively close to India, they are in different IATA traffic conference areas. In an interview, you can say: **“India is TC3, while the UAE is TC2 because the Middle East is included within TC2.”** This demonstrates that you understand the structure rather than memorizing isolated answers."],
["Which Traffic Conference Area does the United States belong to?","The **United States belongs to TC1**.","TC1 broadly covers the Americas and associated Western Hemisphere areas in IATA tariff geography. Therefore, major U.S. destinations such as New York, Los Angeles, Chicago, and Miami are within TC1. A journey from India to the United States can therefore involve travel between TC3 and TC1, potentially via TC2 depending on the routing. Traffic-conference areas can be relevant to fare construction and aviation geography questions. A useful memory sequence is: **USA = TC1, UK/UAE = TC2, India/Japan/Australia = TC3**."],
["Which Traffic Conference Area does the United Kingdom belong to?","The **United Kingdom belongs to TC2** because it is in Europe.","This remains true even though the United Kingdom is not part of the Schengen Area and is no longer a member of the European Union. IATA Traffic Conference Areas, the European Union, and the Schengen Area are three completely different concepts. Interviewers sometimes combine these questions to test whether candidates confuse them. London is in the UK, the UK is geographically in Europe, and for IATA tariff geography it falls within TC2. However, **the UK is not a Schengen member**. Keeping these concepts separate is essential in travel and airline interviews."],
["What is the IATA code for Delhi?","The IATA airport code commonly used for **Indira Gandhi International Airport in Delhi is DEL**.","IATA airport codes are three-letter identifiers widely used in reservations, ticketing, baggage handling, schedules, and passenger communication. DEL is one of the most important Indian codes to memorize for airline interviews. When working with GDS systems, agents frequently use airport or city codes instead of typing complete location names. Other essential Indian codes include BOM for Mumbai, BLR for Bengaluru, HYD for Hyderabad, MAA for Chennai, and CCU for Kolkata. Interviewers may ask airport codes in either direction: “What is Delhi's code?” or “Which city is DEL?”"],
["What is the IATA code for Mumbai?","The IATA code for Mumbai's main international airport is **BOM**.","The code BOM comes from Bombay, the city's former English name, and remains the IATA code despite the city being officially known as Mumbai. This is a useful example showing that airport codes do not always directly match the current city name. In airline interviews, candidates should memorize major domestic and international airport codes because they are commonly used in reservations and ticketing. If asked why Mumbai is BOM rather than MUM, you can explain that airport codes often retain historical identifiers. Other similar cases exist across international aviation."],
["What is the IATA code for Bengaluru?","The IATA code for Bengaluru's Kempegowda International Airport is **BLR**.","BLR is frequently used in airline reservations, baggage tags, flight schedules, and GDS entries. Bengaluru was previously commonly called Bangalore in English, which helps explain the BLR code. For interview preparation, candidates should know major Indian metro codes without hesitation: **DEL – Delhi, BOM – Mumbai, BLR – Bengaluru, HYD – Hyderabad, MAA – Chennai, CCU – Kolkata**. Airport-code questions are usually straightforward, so incorrect answers can unnecessarily reduce confidence in a candidate who claims airline or travel knowledge. Practise them in both directions: code-to-city and city-to-code."],
["What is the IATA code for Hyderabad?","The IATA code for Hyderabad's Rajiv Gandhi International Airport is **HYD**.","HYD is easy to remember because it corresponds closely to the first letters of Hyderabad. In reservation systems, airport codes allow agents to search routes quickly and accurately. A candidate applying for airline customer support, reservations, ticketing, or airport operations should know the major Indian metro codes. In addition to HYD, memorize DEL, BOM, BLR, MAA, and CCU. You should also know important Gulf hubs such as DXB, DOH, and AUH because Indian airline and travel-support roles frequently involve passengers travelling between India and the Gulf region."],
["What is the IATA code for Chennai?","The IATA code for Chennai International Airport is **MAA**.","MAA originates from Madras, the former name of Chennai. Like BOM for Mumbai, it demonstrates how airport codes can retain historical names even after cities are renamed. This is why memorizing airport codes is more reliable than trying to guess them from current city names. For interview preparation, remember **MAA = Chennai**. If an interviewer asks why, you can briefly mention the historical name Madras. Candidates working in reservations or ticketing encounter these codes constantly in itinerary displays, so familiarity with them improves speed and reduces the chance of selecting the wrong airport."],
["What is the IATA code for Kolkata?","The IATA code for Kolkata's Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose International Airport is **CCU**.","CCU is derived from Calcutta, the city's former English name. Similar to BOM and MAA, the airport code remained after the city's official name changed. This makes CCU another important code that cannot be reliably guessed from the modern spelling “Kolkata.” Airline and travel interviewers frequently test these historical codes because they reveal whether a candidate has actually studied aviation geography. Remember the group: **DEL – Delhi, BOM – Mumbai, BLR – Bengaluru, HYD – Hyderabad, MAA – Chennai, CCU – Kolkata.**"],
["What is the IATA code for Dubai International Airport?","The IATA code for **Dubai International Airport is DXB**.","DXB is one of the world's most important international aviation codes and should be memorized by anyone preparing for airline or travel interviews. Do not confuse DXB with DWC, which is associated with Al Maktoum International Airport in Dubai. When a customer simply says “Dubai airport,” an agent should verify the intended airport if the itinerary or service requires precision. DXB is the primary code most passengers associate with Dubai International. In GDS and ticketing work, selecting the wrong airport can completely change the itinerary, fare, or transfer requirements, so airport-code accuracy is critical."],
["What is the IATA code for Doha?","The IATA code for **Hamad International Airport in Doha is DOH**.","DOH is particularly important for candidates preparing for Qatar Airways interviews because Doha is Qatar Airways' main hub. Many Qatar Airways itineraries connect through Doha, such as DEL–DOH–LHR. Agents should therefore understand Doha not only as a destination but also as a major connecting hub. When handling connecting passengers, considerations can include MCT, onward flights, transit requirements, baggage, and disruption management. Remember **DOH = Doha, Qatar**. It is also useful to distinguish the city/airport code DOH from Qatar Airways' two-character airline designator, **QR**."],
["What is the IATA code for London Heathrow?","The IATA code for **London Heathrow Airport is LHR**.","London has several airports, so it is important not to treat “London” and “Heathrow” as interchangeable in every reservation context. Heathrow is LHR, while other London airports have different codes. A passenger asking to travel to London may have a preference based on location, connection, airline, or fare. Agents should therefore confirm the specific airport where necessary. LHR is one of the most frequently tested international codes in airline interviews. Remember that London is in the United Kingdom, the UK belongs to TC2, but the UK is **not** part of the Schengen Area."],
["What is the IATA code for New York JFK Airport?","The IATA code is **JFK**.","JFK refers to John F. Kennedy International Airport in New York. New York is a multi-airport city, so passengers can also use other airports depending on their itinerary. Agents should not assume that every New York booking is automatically JFK. In GDS work, airport and city codes can have different functions, so the requested airport should be confirmed when necessary. JFK is located in the United States, which falls within IATA Traffic Conference Area 1. For interview preparation, remember the association **JFK – New York – USA – TC1**."],
["What is the IATA code for Zurich Airport?","The IATA code for Zurich Airport is **ZRH**.","ZRH is an important European airport code and a common interview geography question. Zurich is in Switzerland. Switzerland is not a member of the European Union, but it **is part of the Schengen Area**. This distinction is useful because interviewers may combine airport-code and visa-geography questions. Also remember that the commonly accepted answer to “What is the capital of Switzerland?” is Bern, not Zurich. Therefore: **ZRH = Zurich Airport; Zurich = Switzerland; Switzerland = Schengen; Bern = Swiss capital.** Keeping these associations together makes them easier to remember."],
["What is the IATA code for Detroit Metropolitan Airport?","The IATA code for Detroit Metropolitan Wayne County Airport is **DTW**.","DTW serves the Detroit metropolitan area in Michigan, United States. Because the United States belongs to IATA TC1, Detroit is also within TC1 for aviation tariff geography. Airport-code questions can appear unexpectedly in reservations interviews, so candidates should learn not only the largest international gateways but also frequently used North American airports. The safest approach is to build groups by region. For example: **JFK – New York, LAX – Los Angeles, DTW – Detroit, YYC – Calgary**. This method makes memorization easier than learning isolated codes."],
["What is the IATA code for Calgary International Airport?","The IATA code for Calgary International Airport is **YYC**.","Calgary is a city in Alberta, Canada. Canadian airport codes often appear less intuitive to candidates because many major codes begin with Y. YYC is therefore worth memorizing rather than trying to derive it from “Calgary.” Canada belongs to IATA TC1. A useful interview association is **YYC – Calgary – Alberta – Canada – TC1**. If an interviewer asks, “Calgary is in which country?” the answer is Canada. If they ask for the province, answer Alberta. Learning airport codes together with their city, country, and TC area gives you stronger aviation geography knowledge."],
["What is Qatar Airways' airline code?","Qatar Airways' two-character IATA airline designator is **QR**.","Airline designators identify carriers in flight numbers and reservation systems. For example, a Qatar Airways flight number begins with QR followed by its flight number. Do not confuse airline codes with airport codes. **QR identifies Qatar Airways**, whereas **DOH identifies Doha/Hamad International Airport**. This distinction sounds basic but is important in GDS work because an incorrect airline or airport code can produce an incorrect search or itinerary. For interview preparation, learn major airline-airport combinations such as **QR–DOH, EK–DXB**, while remembering that operating and marketing carriers can differ on codeshare flights."],
["What is Emirates' airline code?","Emirates' two-character IATA airline designator is **EK**.","EK is used in Emirates flight numbers and reservation displays. Emirates is based in Dubai, and its major hub is Dubai International Airport, whose code is DXB. Therefore, remember the pair **EK = Emirates; DXB = Dubai International**. Airline codes do not always correspond directly to the carrier's first letters, so memorization is necessary. In an interview, do not confuse EK with the airport code. Airline codes identify carriers, while three-letter IATA airport codes identify airports or locations. This distinction is fundamental for anyone claiming GDS or airline reservation knowledge."],
["What is IndiGo's airline code?","IndiGo's two-character IATA airline designator is **6E**.","Unlike many airline designators composed entirely of letters, IndiGo's code combines a number and a letter. It appears before IndiGo flight numbers in reservation systems and passenger itineraries. Candidates with Indian aviation experience should know this immediately. Remember that **6E identifies the airline**, while airport codes such as DEL, BOM, BLR, and HYD identify locations. If an interviewer asks about your previous airline-process experience, knowing these basic identifiers supports your credibility. However, technical knowledge should always be combined with customer-service knowledge such as IROPs, refunds, schedule changes, and escalation handling."],
["What is Air India's airline code?","Air India's two-character IATA airline designator is **AI**.","AI is used in Air India flight numbers and airline reservation displays. It is one of the easiest airline codes to remember because it closely matches the airline's name. However, candidates should still distinguish the airline designator from airport and aircraft codes. In interview preparation, learn major carriers in groups: **AI – Air India, 6E – IndiGo, QR – Qatar Airways, EK – Emirates, LH – Lufthansa**. Airline codes become particularly important when reading codeshare itineraries because the marketing carrier's code may differ from the airline physically operating the aircraft."],
["How many countries are currently in the Schengen Area?","The Schengen Area currently consists of **29 countries**.","This is an important update because older study materials may still say 26 or 27. Bulgaria and Romania became full Schengen members from January 1, 2025, bringing the total to 29. The Schengen Area allows travel without routine internal border checks between participating countries, although temporary controls can still occur. Do not confuse Schengen membership with European Union membership. Some Schengen countries, such as Switzerland and Norway, are not EU members, while Ireland is an EU member but is not part of Schengen. For airline interviews, remember the current number: **29**."],
["Name at least 10 Schengen countries.","France, Germany, Italy, Spain, Portugal, Netherlands, Belgium, Austria, Switzerland, and Greece.","You should know more than ten in case the interviewer asks follow-up questions. The Schengen Area currently includes 29 countries. Other examples include Norway, Sweden, Denmark, Finland, Iceland, Poland, Czechia, Hungary, Croatia, Slovenia, Slovakia, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, Luxembourg, Malta, Liechtenstein, Bulgaria, and Romania. Remember that **the United Kingdom and Ireland are not Schengen members**. For an airline interview, the purpose is usually to test basic travel geography rather than expect you to recite every immigration rule. Visa requirements for an actual passenger should always be checked through authoritative sources."],
["Are Bulgaria and Romania part of the Schengen Area?","**Yes. Bulgaria and Romania are full members of the Schengen Area.**","Their integration occurred in stages, and controls at internal air and sea borders were removed earlier. Internal land-border controls were then lifted from **January 1, 2025**, completing their full participation in the Schengen Area. This is why older interview notes listing 27 Schengen countries are outdated. Candidates working with travel information need to be careful with changing visa and border rules. Even though knowing the current number is useful for interviews, actual passenger-document advice should always be verified using current authoritative immigration or travel-document sources rather than relying solely on memorized information."],
["Is the United Kingdom part of the Schengen Area?","**No. The United Kingdom is not part of the Schengen Area.**","This is important because passengers sometimes assume that all major European destinations use the same border and visa arrangements. The United Kingdom operates its own immigration and border policies. Therefore, a Schengen visa does not automatically mean that a passenger can enter the UK. The passenger's nationality, documents, itinerary, and current UK requirements must be checked separately. In an airline interview, the simple answer is “No,” but demonstrating the distinction between **Europe, the European Union, and Schengen** shows stronger travel knowledge. London is geographically in Europe and TC2, but the UK is not Schengen."],
["Is Ireland part of the Schengen Area?","**No. Ireland is not part of the Schengen Area.**","Ireland is a member of the European Union but does not participate in the Schengen border-free area. This is an excellent example showing why the EU and Schengen should not be treated as the same thing. Passenger visa and entry requirements therefore need to be checked specifically for Ireland. In interviews, you may be asked to identify countries that are European but not Schengen or countries that are Schengen but not EU members. Remember: **Ireland = EU but not Schengen; Switzerland = Schengen but not EU**. This distinction is particularly useful in international travel-support roles."],
["What is the capital of Switzerland?","**Bern** is the standard answer.","Many candidates incorrectly answer Zurich or Geneva because these cities are internationally famous and important for business and aviation. However, Bern is the city generally treated as Switzerland's capital for interview and general-knowledge purposes; technically, Switzerland describes Bern as its “federal city.” Zurich is Switzerland's largest city and a major financial and aviation center, while Geneva is another major international city. For aviation preparation, remember: **Bern = federal city/capital answer; Zurich Airport = ZRH; Switzerland = Schengen but not EU.** Connecting related facts makes them easier to remember for interviews."],
["How many continents are there?","Under the commonly taught model, there are **seven continents**: Asia, Africa, North America, South America, Antarctica, Europe, and Australia/Oceania.","Different geographical traditions sometimes use different continent models, but the seven-continent model is the standard answer expected in most Indian and international job interviews. Aviation candidates should have basic world geography because airlines operate across countries, regions, and time zones. Knowing continents also helps when learning IATA Traffic Conference Areas, although the two systems are not identical. Do not say TC1, TC2, and TC3 correspond directly to three continents. They are aviation tariff-geography regions. For interview purposes, confidently answer **seven** and be prepared to name all seven."],
["What are the Seven Sister States of India?","The Seven Sister States are **Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, and Tripura**.","These seven states are located in Northeast India and are commonly grouped under the term “Seven Sisters.” Sikkim is also a northeastern state but is not one of the original Seven Sisters. This question may appear in airline interviews because aviation roles often test Indian geography along with international geography and airport codes. A useful way to prepare is to know major cities and airports in different regions as well. If asked only for the Seven Sisters, list the seven states clearly and do not add Sikkim to the original group."],
["What is a one-way journey?","A one-way journey takes a passenger from an origin to a destination without a booked return to the original point as part of that journey.","For example, **DEL–DXB** is a simple one-way journey from Delhi to Dubai. A one-way itinerary can still contain connections; for example, DEL–DOH–LHR can still be one-way because the passenger travels from Delhi to London without returning to Delhi. Therefore, “one-way” does not necessarily mean “one flight.” It refers to the overall journey structure. Fare construction, visa requirements, and entry requirements may differ depending on the itinerary. In customer support, agents should verify the passenger's complete journey rather than assuming that one-way travel means a single nonstop segment."],
["What is a round trip?","A round trip generally involves travelling from an origin to another point and returning to the original origin.","A simple example is **DEL–DXB–DEL**. The passenger starts in Delhi, travels to Dubai, and later returns to Delhi. A round trip can contain connecting flights, so it does not necessarily mean there are only two flight segments. Fare construction has technical definitions that can be more specific, but for a general interview this explanation is sufficient. When comparing it with a one-way journey, emphasize that the passenger returns to the starting point. When comparing it with a circle trip, emphasize that a circle trip can involve multiple intermediate destinations or different routing before returning to the origin."],
["What is the difference between a round trip and a circle trip?","A round trip generally travels from an origin to a destination and back, while a circle trip returns to the original point through a journey involving multiple points or different routing.","A simple round-trip example is **DEL–DXB–DEL**. A simplified circle-trip example could be **DEL–DXB–LHR–DEL**, where the passenger travels through several points before returning to Delhi. Technical fare construction can involve more detailed definitions, but this distinction is suitable for basic interview preparation. Remember: **round trip = outward journey and return; circle trip = journey around multiple points that eventually returns to origin**. If applying for a fare-construction role, study the formal tariff definitions in greater depth."],
["What is DGCA?","DGCA stands for the **Directorate General of Civil Aviation**, India's civil aviation regulator.","DGCA performs regulatory and safety oversight functions within Indian civil aviation under the applicable legal framework. Candidates applying for Indian airline or airport roles should know this abbreviation because DGCA requirements affect many areas of aviation operations, safety, licensing, and compliance. Do not confuse DGCA with an airline, airport operator, or travel agency. In an interview, a concise answer is: **“DGCA is India's civil aviation regulatory authority responsible for regulatory oversight and aviation safety functions.”** If asked about a specific DGCA regulation, avoid guessing; identify the relevant current regulation or Civil Aviation Requirement."],
["What is CAR in Indian aviation?","CAR stands for **Civil Aviation Requirements**.","Civil Aviation Requirements are regulatory requirements issued by DGCA covering specified areas of Indian civil aviation. Different CARs address different subjects, so there is no single generic “DGCA CAR policy” that answers every passenger-service question. If an interviewer asks about a particular CAR, first identify the subject—for example, passenger rights, operations, safety, or another regulatory area—and then explain the applicable requirement. This is especially important because regulations can be amended. In professional work, agents should refer to the current official DGCA requirement and their airline's approved procedures rather than relying on old training notes or memory."],
["A passenger wants to change the date of an international ticket. What will you do?","I would retrieve the reservation, verify the passenger and ticket details, understand the requested new date, and check availability and fare conditions.","I would review the ticket status, original itinerary, fare rules, change restrictions, no-show status where relevant, and new flight availability. I would then determine the applicable fare difference, penalty, taxes, or additional collection according to the authorized ticketing process. Before changing anything, I would explain the available option and total applicable amount to the passenger and obtain agreement. After confirmation, I would perform the authorized rebooking and reissue or revalidation process, save the booking, verify the updated ticket and itinerary, and provide the passenger with the new confirmation."],
["A passenger and spouse are travelling together, but the spouse becomes sick and wants to cancel while the other passenger still wants to travel. What will you do?","I would first verify the booking, passenger details, tickets, and cancellation conditions. If the passengers are in the same PNR and require separate servicing, I would follow the approved procedure to divide or split the relevant passenger.","After the split, I would carefully verify both reservation records. I would keep the travelling passenger's itinerary intact and then handle the sick passenger's reservation separately. Before cancellation or refund, I would check the fare conditions, ticket status, applicable penalties, and whether any medical exception or waiver exists under airline policy. I would explain the eligible options before processing anything. Finally, I would verify both PNRs and tickets to ensure the travelling passenger remains correctly booked and the other passenger's request has been handled accurately."]];